{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's discriminating evidence conflates 'rule content is causally necessary' with 'genuine reliability gain.' Consider a checklist whose rules are correct but only weakly diagnostic, while the panel's true advantage comes from aggregating independent judgments (wisdom-of-crowds). On the corrupted-checklist control, performance would indeed drop sharply on the divergence stratum because the wrong rules actively mislead—satisfying the target's criterion for a 'genuine reliability gain'—yet the original checklist's rule content was not the actual source of reliability; the drop occurs simply because inverted wrong rules are worse than weak-but-correct rules. The target's interaction-effect criterion cannot distinguish a checklist that genuinely carries epistemic content from one that merely avoids being actively harmful, so it fails to identify the evidence that separates a genuine reliability gain from mere compliance.","counterexample":[{"implication":"The target classifies this as a genuine reliability gain attributable to checklist rule content because performance drops sharply when corrupted. But the real checklist actually underperforms pure aggregation, and the drop is caused by active misinformation, not by loss of genuine epistemic content. The target's criterion falsely certifies a non-epistemic compliance pattern as genuine.","setup":"Panel of 20 non-experts uses a weak checklist with one marginally diagnostic rule (e.g., 'favor answer A when cue X present,' where cue X has 55% diagnostic accuracy). Expert intuition achieves 60% accuracy. Panel aggregation alone (no checklist) achieves 75%. Real-checklist condition: 73% (checklist slightly distracts from aggregation). Corrupted-checklist condition: 45% (inverted rule systematically misleads). Divergence stratum: real-checklist 70%, corrupted-checklist 30%.","target_alias":"SRC_001"}],"target":"e911707888498675480e564d4c2d39b972c06c8f9eba7a37f7e85e895d8ec94f"}]}